7
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:34,280
For the past few tutorials, we've been looking at some different separation techniques that

8
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:36,320
we can perform in the laboratory.

9
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:41,000
These techniques separate the components of a mixture by taking advantage of differences

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,720
in their physical and chemical properties.

11
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:48,240
Today we are going to look at another technique that is more of a purification technique.

12
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:52,380
Sometimes when performing a chemical reaction, a solid will be produced.

13
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:57,620
We saw this occur when we performed an extraction, and while working with the aqueous layer, we

14
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:00,760
caused benzoic acid to precipitate.

15
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:05,980
We then separated this precipitate from the rest of the solution by using vacuum filtration.

16
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:11,460
However, we did not discuss the fact that when a solid precipitates, it may incorporate

17
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:15,560
a number of impurities from solution into the lattice structure.

18
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:20,460
In order to separate the product from these impurities, or to purify the solid, we will

19
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:24,780
perform something called recrystallization.

20
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:28,960
Recrystallization is a technique that takes advantage of the difference in solubility between

21
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:33,620
our desired product and any impurities that may be present within.

22
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:38,320
The way this works is that we will be taking our impure product and dissolving it once

23
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:43,960
more in a hot solvent so that it will dissociate and release the impurities.

24
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:49,520
The solution is then cooled down, which causes our pure product to crystallize again, but

25
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:55,060
this time the impurities remain dissolved in the solvent, because of their enhanced solubility

26
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:58,840
in that solvent over the product we are isolating.

27
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:03,380
The solid will initially dissolve because of the higher temperature, since solubility

28
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:08,740
typically increases with temperature, but as it cools, solubility is reduced, until

29
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:14,340
it eventually crashes out of solution once more, this time with negligible impurity.

30
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:17,860
To carry out a recrystallization, we will need a few materials.

31
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:20,560
First, we will need two beakers.

32
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:26,640
We will use a 250 milliliter and a 150 milliliter beaker in this experiment.

33
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:31,120
Then we will need a hot plate with a magnetic stirrer, or a Bunsen burner if a hot plate

34
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:32,400
is not available.

35
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:41,200
We will also need a stir bar, stirring rod, a vacuum filtration setup, a powder funnel,

36
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:45,000
a watch glass, and disposable pipettes.

37
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:48,100
Lastly, we're going to need our product.

38
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:53,620
Remember, this is a technique that is typically applied in order to purify a solid product

39
00:02:53,620 --> 00:02:56,820
that has been collected at the end of a chemical reaction.

40
00:02:56,820 --> 00:03:01,500
In this case, we're going to use impure benzoic acid, as though we are continuing

41
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,540
the extraction we performed earlier in the series.

42
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:07,940
And for the solvent, we will use distilled water.

43
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:13,940
Now choosing a suitable recrystallization solvent is the most important part of the experiment.

44
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:18,840
We need our impurities to be soluble in our solvent at all temperatures.

45
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:25,280
They must dissolve as we heat things up, and remain dissolved when we cool it back down.

46
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:30,080
However, the product must only be soluble at high temperatures.

47
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:35,880
It dissolves when we heat it up, but then precipitates again as we cool it back down.

48
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:41,080
To find an adequate solvent, we must look at tabulated solubility data, and compare

49
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:46,120
the solubilities of our product in different solvents at different temperatures.

50
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:50,300
You can find this data online, since many different compounds and solvents have been

51
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:52,220
studied in this manner.

52
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:57,420
In this case, a quick search will tell us that benzoic acid has a very high solubility

53
00:03:57,420 --> 00:04:04,540
in boiling water, 56.31 grams per liter, while it is very low in room temperature water,

54
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:07,540
only 3.44 grams per liter.

55
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:13,480
This makes water a good choice for our recrystallization solvent, and specifically distilled water,

56
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:17,720
as we do not want to introduce additional impurities from tap water.

57
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:20,220
We are ready to begin the recrystallization.

58
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:26,040
We will start by heating up 150 milliliters of pure distilled water in a 250 milliliter

59
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,040
beaker on a hot plate.

60
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:31,160
As that heats up, let's weigh our impure product.

61
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:35,000
We're going to be using 3 grams of benzoic acid today.

62
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:40,220
Let's add the powder to a clean, dry, 150 milliliter beaker.

63
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:45,020
Once the water is sufficiently heated, we're going to use a disposable pipette to add

64
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:47,820
hot water to our product.

65
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:53,060
Based on the solubility of benzoic acid, it looks like we should add around 100 milliliters

66
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:59,180
of water to our 3 grams of benzoic acid to ensure that it will completely dissolve.

67
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:03,740
We can add a little bit of hot water at a time, slowly, until we can see that it

68
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:05,680
has fully dissolved.

69
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:10,720
Add a stir bar to the solution and place it on the hot plate alongside the hot water beaker,

70
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:14,640
so that you can keep heating and dissolving as you add more water.

71
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:19,160
While we do need the solid to dissolve completely, it is important to use the minimum amount of

72
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:22,440
solvent that is possible during a recrystallization.

73
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:25,420
Do not add more solvent than is needed.

74
00:05:25,420 --> 00:05:29,380
After everything is dissolved, remove the stirring rod and stir bar.

75
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:35,800
Before you let the solution cool down to crystallize, check for any impurities that did not dissolve,

76
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:40,500
as some impurities may be insoluble in your solvent at any temperature.

77
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:44,820
You will have to filter them out using vacuum or gravity filtration.

78
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:49,960
Make sure you do this immediately before the solution cools down, or you risk filtering

79
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:52,280
away some of your product.

80
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:56,220
This is why we call this step hot filtration.

81
00:05:56,220 --> 00:06:00,460
After you have made sure that your solution is clear, leave it at room temperature and

82
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:04,680
cover it with a watch glass, allowing it to cool down slowly.

83
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:09,960
The crystals should start to form relatively quickly, and as more cooling occurs, more

84
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:12,040
crystals will appear.

85
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:17,280
Be patient and do not move or shake the beaker while the cooling is happening.

86
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:22,120
To complete the recrystallization, you can move your beaker to an ice bath or a fridge

87
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:24,060
for five minutes or so.

88
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:27,740
This is to ensure that all our product has precipitated.

89
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:33,460
The impurities are now in the liquid solution, and these lovely crystals represent our pure

90
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:34,460
product.

91
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:37,840
We're now going to separate the crystals from the liquid.

92
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:43,120
Use a stirring rod to mix the solution a bit so that we can pour it into the filter.

93
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:49,240
We will be using our vacuum filtration setup, but gravity filtration is also possible.

94
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:53,700
Pour your solution into the Buchner funnel, and the crystals will be collected.

95
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:59,440
It is important to pour more pure solvent over the crystals during filtration, as this will

96
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:06,140
wash your product, meaning that it will remove the original solvent since it contains impurities,

97
00:07:06,140 --> 00:07:09,560
and we don't want any of those sitting on our pure crystals.

98
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:12,320
We must wash them through the funnel.

99
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:17,140
After this, let the vacuum run for a few minutes to further dry your product.

100
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:19,840
Your product should now be almost dry.

101
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:24,160
You can leave it out to air dry at room temperature or put it in the oven.

102
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:28,080
You have now completed a recrystallization.

103
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:31,720
Now the point of this technique was to get pure crystals.

104
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:37,300
The best way to test the purity of a solid is by measuring its melting point and comparing

105
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:42,780
it to the original impure mixture, as well as the literature value for the product.

106
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:48,120
To perform melting point analysis, we need a melting point tube, and we need to insert

107
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:50,540
a tiny sample of our solid.

108
00:07:50,540 --> 00:07:54,760
We then need to use some mechanical force to get this sample to the bottom of the melting

109
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:56,340
point tube.

110
00:07:56,340 --> 00:08:01,520
Then we place it in the melting point apparatus, turn it on, and wait for the temperature

111
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:05,660
to slowly approach the expected melting point range.

112
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:09,060
As it nears, begin to watch through the eyepiece.

113
00:08:09,060 --> 00:08:14,080
As soon as you see the first signs of melting, record the temperature.

114
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:18,880
Then the moment you see that all of the sample is completely melted, record the temperature

115
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:20,240
again.

116
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:23,080
That is your melting point range.

117
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:28,060
The purified sample should give a melting point range that is much closer to the reported

118
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:32,200
value when compared with the impure, crude sample.

119
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:37,360
In addition, an impure solid will give a wide range during melting point analysis, meaning

120
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:41,860
it will start and finish melting several degrees apart or more.

121
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:47,240
A highly pure solid will have a very sharp melting point, where all of it melts at a

122
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:50,040
particular temperature very rapidly.

123
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:55,020
So the width of the range, together with a comparison to the tabulated melting point,

124
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:59,520
will allow us to gauge the purity of the recrystallized product.

125
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:04,000
Be sure to turn off the melting point apparatus when you are finished with it.

126
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,680
We can also check the percent recovery of our sample by weighing the purified sample.

127
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:13,860
Before the mass of the crystals recovered, we can divide this by the mass of the original

128
00:09:13,860 --> 00:09:18,360
sample and multiply by a hundred to get the percent recovery.

129
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:23,520
During recrystallization we always lose a percentage of our sample, so expect for this value to

130
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:25,640
be less than 100%.

131
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:30,740
This is due to the impurities which have been removed from the lattice, as well as some

132
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:34,100
of the pure product which is lost in the process.

133
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:36,560
There is one more thing to keep in mind.

134
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:41,520
Sometimes your crystals will refuse to grow, even if you use an ice bath to cool them down

135
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:43,160
to a lower temperature.

136
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:47,920
This can be caused by the glass surface in the beaker being too smooth.

137
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:53,860
One solution is to use a glass rod to scrape your beaker on the bottom and sides.

138
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:59,280
This will generate some loose glass particles upon which your crystals can grow.

139
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:04,240
Another good method to initiate crystallization is to add a few crystals of your pure product

140
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:09,200
to the solution so that other molecules can join an existing lattice.

141
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:12,240
This is called using a seed crystal.

142
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:17,640
In some more advanced recrystallizations, we may need to use multisolvent systems.

143
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:22,240
In this case, this first solvent dissolves the impure product, while the addition of

144
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:26,900
a second solvent causes the pure product to gradually crystallize.

145
00:10:26,900 --> 00:10:30,520
But as that's a bit more advanced, this will be all for recrystallization.

146
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:32,920
Let's move on to some other techniques.
